+# Accounts service FMEA
+
+## Scope and scoring
+
+This assessment covers the Spring Boot accounts API, its H2 persistence, scheduled payment dispatch, security boundary,
+and the scheme gateway, fraud service, core ledger and notification service. It is based on the code, `README.md` and
+the OKF bundle as of 4 November 2026. The service is the source of the customer's view of accounts, but the core ledger
+is the source of truth for money.
+
+Scores are current-state scores on a 1–10 scale. Severity (S) is the impact if the failure occurs; occurrence (O) is
+the expected likelihood under normal operation; detection (D) is the likelihood that the failure escapes the existing
+controls (10 = difficult to detect). RPN = S × O × D. These are prioritisation scores, not probabilities. Any item with
+S ≥ 9 or RPN ≥ 300 should have an owner and release decision before go-live.
+
+## Failure mode register
+
+| ID | Failure mode | Likely cause | Effect on customers, dependent systems and teams | Current detection / controls | S | O | D | RPN | Recommended action |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| F-01 | An unscreened payment is sent | `PaymentService` does not call the available fraud client, despite FRD-7 requiring screening before every payment | Fraudulent or sanctioned payments can leave the bank; fraud team loses its screening queue; compliance and reconciliation face a material incident | Fraud client exists and tests can fake it, but no runtime control or alert proves a decision preceded submission | 10 | 6 | 9 | **540** | Release-blocking: screen synchronously before scheme submission; fail closed on timeout/error; persist decision and correlation ID; map `ACCEPT`, `REFER` to `HELD_FOR_REVIEW`, and `REJECT` to `REJECTED`; add contract and negative-path tests and an alert for any submitted payment without a decision |
+| F-02 | One customer action creates two payments | POST has no idempotency key or uniqueness constraint; a client retry after a timeout or ambiguous response creates a new payment ID and scheme reference | Customer can be debited twice; scheme settlement and finance reconciliation break; support and operations must investigate a duplicate | Daily-limit check and unique scheme reference per payment ID do not prevent duplicate requests; no duplicate-payment metric | 10 | 5 | 8 | **400** | Require an idempotency key on payment creation, store it per tenant/account with an immutable request hash, return the original result on replay, and reject conflicting reuse; test concurrent retries |
+| F-03 | Scheme accepts a payment but the API loses the local record | Scheme timeout is not caught; the transaction rolls back even though the gateway may have accepted `P` + payment ID | Customer sees an error and may retry; money may leave without a visible payment; reconciliation sees scheme settlement with no local record; operations cannot reliably recover it | 10-second client timeout; no status query or timeout recovery; daily scheme-to-ledger reconciliation may find a break | 10 | 4 | 9 | **360** | Catch timeout as `SUBMISSION_UNKNOWN`; query by the same reference, or reconcile asynchronously before retrying; make status transitions durable and alert on unknown submissions; never issue a new scheme reference for recovery |
+| F-04 | Scheme and ledger disagree after scheme submission | Ledger call fails or times out after scheme acceptance; the local account copy is debited and payment is marked `POSTING_FAILED` | Payment may be sent but not booked, leaving the customer balance and core ledger inconsistent; finance reconciliation and operations must repair it; customer status is ambiguous | `POSTING_FAILED` is persisted and logged; operations are expected to use a daily exception report; no automatic reconciliation is shown | 10 | 5 | 6 | **300** | Use an outbox/reconciliation workflow; retain the original `ledgerReference`; query/repost idempotently, never generate a new reference; expose age/SLA alerts and a repair runbook; keep customer-facing status explicit |
+| F-05 | A timed-out ledger post is retried as a new posting | Operator or future retry code treats `LedgerTimeoutException` as definite failure and generates another UUID instead of reusing `ledgerReference` | Core ledger can debit twice; customer's money and finance books are wrong; reconciliation and operations incur a high-severity manual incident | Ledger is idempotent only for the same reference for 24 hours; code stores a reference but has no enforced uniqueness or retry path | 10 | 3 | 8 | **240** | Make `ledgerReference` immutable and unique; add a reconciliation command that always reuses it and first checks ledger status; add a test for timeout-after-booking and duplicate retry |
+| F-06 | Payment is rejected because of a bank holiday or is sent on the wrong date | `WorkingDays` knows only Monday–Friday; the queue runs only `MON-FRI`, while the scheme rejects bank holidays with `DT01` | Customer's payment is delayed or rejected unexpectedly; app status, scheme settlement, support and operations disagree; queued-payment SLA is missed | Execution date and queued status are visible; scheme rejection records `DT01`; no bank-holiday calendar or overdue alert is implemented | 8 | 6 | 7 | **336** | Use an authoritative UK bank-holiday calendar shared with the scheme; calculate next working day centrally; run due processing every day or via a durable scheduler; alert on `QUEUED` past date and test holidays, DST and restarts |
+| F-07 | Queued payments remain stuck | Scheduler is disabled, crashes, has no durable lock/retry, or a deployment occurs at 06:00; the job has no explicit recovery path | Payments miss their promised execution date; customers, scheme, fraud and operations queues accumulate | `QueuedPaymentJob` selects due rows and operations are told to report overdue rows, but no metric, lock, retry or alert is defined | 9 | 5 | 8 | **360** | Replace best-effort in-process scheduling with a durable queue/claim mechanism; make dispatch idempotent; publish counts and oldest age for `QUEUED`; page on due payments past SLA and run a restart/replay drill |
+| F-08 | Fraud `REFER` is treated as a rejection or never reaches review | The review workflow and customer notification are not implemented in the payment path; `HELD_FOR_REVIEW` is only an enum/documented state | Legitimate payments are denied or held indefinitely; fraud team misses its four-hour review target; customers receive the wrong outcome and complaints increase | Enum and fraud fake cover the intended states, but there is no persisted screening result, review endpoint/inbox integration or timer alert | 9 | 5 | 8 | **360** | Persist screening outcome and review deadline; publish held payments to the fraud work queue; implement approve/reject transitions with segregation of duties, `PAYMENT_UNDER_REVIEW`, and four-hour ageing alerts |
+| F-09 | Balance copy permits an overdraft or rejects a valid payment | Daytime balance is a copy, is overwritten only at 02:00, or concurrent requests both pass the check; it is not reserved atomically | Customer sees incorrect available balance; multiple payments can exceed funds/daily limit; ledger, app and finance differ | Nightly ledger overwrite is documented; per-request balance and daily-limit checks exist, but no locking/reservation or real-time ledger check for payment creation | 9 | 6 | 7 | **378** | Treat available funds and daily limits as a concurrency-controlled reservation, or authoritatively check the ledger; add optimistic/pessimistic locking and concurrent-payment tests; reconcile copy to ledger and alert on drift |
+| F-10 | Account closes with funds still held | Closure request flow does not show an approval/decision implementation or a ledger zero-balance check; a copied balance can be stale | Customer funds can be stranded; closure, finance and operations need manual remediation; regulatory/customer complaint risk rises | Requests are pending and approver-list access is tested; there is no evidence of ledger check, decision transition or close transaction | 10 | 3 | 9 | **270** | Implement an explicit approve/reject state machine; before approval query the ledger for zero balance and pending postings; atomically close only after the check; audit approver, ledger result and timestamp; alert on aged pending requests |
+| F-11 | Tenant or role boundary exposes another customer's data or permits an unauthorised payment | Incorrect/missing JWT claims, a future endpoint bypasses `AccessPolicy`, or tenant filtering is omitted in a repository query | Cross-tenant data breach or unauthorised payment; identity, security, support and regulator response required | Signed JWT, URL/method security, resource-level policy, and tests for cross-tenant 404s exist; no production authorization audit or automated endpoint matrix is described | 10 | 2 | 8 | **160** | Add deny-by-default integration tests for every endpoint and role/scope/tenant combination; centralise tenant predicates; audit authorization decisions without sensitive data; alert on anomalous cross-tenant 403/404 patterns and review key rotation/claim validation |
+| F-12 | Internal notes, risk data or comments are returned to a customer | DTO/repository expansion accidentally serializes staff-only entities, or a new controller omits the customer/staff projection boundary | Confidential fraud/risk and complaint information leaks; data-protection incident and loss of customer trust | Existing comments document staff-only intent and controllers use projections, but there is no response schema contract or DLP test described | 9 | 2 | 8 | **144** | Keep separate customer/staff DTOs; enforce field-level contract tests and response snapshots; add sensitive-field scanning to CI and security review for every endpoint |
+| F-13 | Notification failure makes the customer unaware of payment state | Notification service is down, slow or returns an error; notifications are best-effort with no retry | Payment can complete while the customer receives no confirmation; support contacts and disputes rise, though payment and books should remain intact | Failure is caught and logged; no retry, delivery status, dead-letter queue or customer-status fallback is present | 6 | 5 | 7 | **210** | Emit an outbox event after the durable payment transition; retry with backoff and a dead-letter queue; monitor delivery lag/failure rate; preserve the rule that notification failure never rolls back money movement |
+| F-14 | Notification contains excessive account data or fails unpredictably | Template mapping changes, or the assumed account-number length makes `substring(4)` unsafe; full account number/sort code is prohibited | Full account number in a message is a reportable data-protection breach; malformed data can suppress confirmation | Current code sends only `accountEnding`; exception is caught and logged; no schema validation or outbound payload audit | 9 | 2 | 8 | **144** | Make notification contracts typed and validate last-four derivation at the boundary; redact/log only identifiers; add tests that assert no full account or sort code can leave the service; monitor template/schema violations |
+| F-15 | Service restarts and loses all account/payment state | Production datasource is `jdbc:h2:mem:accounts` with `create-drop`; it is not durable across process restart and is unsuitable as the system's operational store | Accounts, payments, cases, statements and closure records disappear; customers and every dependent team lose continuity; regulatory/complaint records are unavailable | Startup fixture loading and API tests prove boot behavior, not durability; health endpoint does not prove data persistence | 10 | 3 | 10 | **300** | Release-blocking: use a managed durable database with migrations, backups, restore tests, HA and encryption; remove production fixtures; define RPO/RTO and verify restart/failover recovery |
+| F-16 | Statements are missing or incorrect for regulatory reporting | Statement data is fixture-backed and a repository exists, but no statement generation/serving workflow is exposed; ledger closing balances may not be captured at month-end | Customers cannot obtain a correct statement; monthly regulatory return due by the fifth working day is wrong or late; finance/reporting must reconstruct data | Seed statements exist and repository can list them; no scheduled month-end job, ledger snapshot validation, completeness check or reporting alert is evident | 9 | 4 | 9 | **324** | Build a durable month-end statement process from ledger snapshots; make it rerunnable and immutable; validate every account/period, reconcile totals, publish completeness metrics, and alert before the fifth-working-day deadline |
+| F-17 | API or scheduled processing is unavailable or overloads dependencies | Instance/database failure, unbounded list queries, slow external calls, or no bulkhead/back-pressure around the 2/5/10-second integrations | Mobile, online banking and back office fail or time out; queued work may pile up and dependent teams receive partial service | Target availability is 99.9%; integration timeouts and `/actuator/health` exist, but no rate limits, circuit breakers, readiness/dependency checks or SLO dashboards are shown | 8 | 4 | 8 | **256** | Use a durable production DB, connection/request limits, circuit breakers and bulkheads; define readiness versus liveness; instrument latency/error/saturation per endpoint and dependency; page on SLO burn and queue age; load-test payment and list endpoints |
+| F-18 | Date/time rules produce wrong cut-off, execution or audit dates | UK clock is not used consistently, DST changes are mishandled, or `15:30` boundary semantics differ from the scheme; bank holidays are absent | Payment is sent late/early or queued incorrectly; customer promise, scheme settlement and audit trail disagree | Injected UK `Clock` and a cut-off configuration are good controls; tests cover a normal weekday/weekend but not DST, exact boundary or holidays | 8 | 4 | 7 | **224** | Define cut-off inclusivity with the scheme; use `Instant` plus explicit Europe/London conversion; centralise calendar logic; add boundary, DST, holiday and clock-skew tests and telemetry of calculated execution date |
+| F-19 | Operational exception is not detected or cannot be investigated | Logs are mostly WARN/root WARN, there is no evidenced metrics/tracing/audit stream, and `POSTING_FAILED`/overdue queue handling depends on an external report | Reconciliation breaks, held/failed payments age silently, and operations cannot prove what happened or meet response deadlines | Some payment IDs and stack traces are logged; status fields and IDs exist; no documented dashboard, alert thresholds, correlation IDs or immutable audit trail | 9 | 6 | 9 | **486** | Establish structured audit events for every payment transition and authorization decision; add correlation IDs, dependency metrics, dashboards and pages for unknown submission, posting failure, held-age, overdue queue, ledger drift and notification DLQ; retain searchable records under access control |
+
+## Release priorities
+
+1. Do not release payment capability until F-01, F-02, F-03, F-04/F-05 and F-15 have owners, tests and an approved
+   operational recovery path. These can cause fraud, duplicate debits, untraceable money movement or total data loss.
+2. Next, implement F-06 through F-10 and F-16: durable scheduling, bank-holiday handling, fraud review, atomic funds
+   control, ledger-verified closure and statement completeness are customer and regulatory commitments.
+3. Make F-11 through F-14 and F-17 through F-19 part of the release exit criteria: security regression coverage,
+   privacy-safe notifications, observability and dependency resilience are needed to detect and contain the failures
+   above.
+
+## Verification baseline
+
+`mvn -o -q test` passes for the repository's existing API tests. Those tests cover normal payment, scheme rejection,
+ledger timeout status, queue dispatch, access control and basic scheduling, but they do not demonstrate the release
+controls identified above—particularly fraud invocation, idempotent request replay, ambiguous scheme outcomes, bank
+holidays, persistence across restart, closure approval/ledger checks, statement generation or production alerting.
